---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar, line } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const century = assertion('lima-grew-fivefold-after-the-oil')

const peaks = assertion('three-peaks-thirty-years-apart')

const townships = assertion('every-township-grew')

const outside = assertion('the-plants-are-outside-every-city')

// Eight counts at uneven spacing — twenty years to 1920, ten thereafter. Still a bar chart
// rather than a line: a line drawn across categories of unequal width states a rate the
// spacing does not support, and the 1880-to-1900 step is twice the others.
const centurySpec = bar(century.figures.map((f) => f.label), century.figures.map((f) => f.value), {
  yTitle: 'Residents of Lima',
  labelValues: true,
})

// Nine decennial counts a decade apart, so a line is right here where it was wrong above:
// the spacing is even and every point is an enumeration rather than an estimate.
const peaksSpec = line(peaks.figures.map((f) => f.label), [{ name: 'Allen County outside Lima', y: peaks.figures.map((f) => f.value) }], {
  yTitle: 'Residents',
  labelLast: true,
})
---

<Article slug="lima-before-the-series-begins">
  <p>
    Everything above begins in 1970, because 1970 was the earliest figure this corpus held. The
    city tripled in the twenty years after the 1885 oil strike and doubled again by 1920,
    reaching 41,326 people, and went on rising at every census to 53,734 in 1970. It is 35,579
    today. Every count before 1970 is higher than the one before it and every count after is
    lower, so 1970 is the peak and not merely the earliest thing on the page.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={century} />

  <ChartFigure
    id="century"
    title="Lima, 1880 to 1970"
    caption="From the Census Bureau's 1960 volume, which carries each place back to its earliest census. Three county histories supplied the 1880, 1900 and 1920 figures first; the Census confirms the first two to the person and corrects the third from 41,306 to 41,326. The steps are twenty years to 1920 and ten after it, which is why these are bars and not a line."
    columns={['Census', 'Residents']}
    rows={century.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
    height={280}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="century" set:html={JSON.stringify(centurySpec)} is:inline />

  <h2>Three peaks, thirty years apart</h2>
  <p>
    A line labelled "Allen County" hides the thing this page is actually about. Take Lima out of
    the county and what is left grew at every census from 1940 to 2000 — from 28,592 people to
    68,157 — and has fallen at both censuses since. So the city turned in 1970, the county in
    1980, and the county outside the city in 2000. For thirty of those years the county was
    shrinking while two thirds of it grew. That is not in tension with the section above: the
    ring has been falling since 2000, and the four recent years measured there sit inside the
    falling part of a curve that rose for sixty.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={peaks} />

  <ChartFigure
    id="peaks"
    title="Allen County outside Lima, 1940–2020"
    caption="Subtraction, not a published figure: the city's count taken from the county's at each census, both from Census publications. The one fall before 2000 is 208 people in the 1980s, and the 1980s are the one decade in which Lima is known to have annexed — which moves people out of this series without anyone moving house."
    columns={['Census', 'Residents outside Lima']}
    rows={peaks.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="peaks" set:html={JSON.stringify(peaksSpec)} is:inline />

  <p>
    The same thing said one grain down. The county's twelve townships are now on record for 1930,
    1940 and 1950, and every one of them holds more people today than it did in 1930 — from Sugar
    Creek, a fifth larger, to Shawnee, six times over. Shawnee and American are the two that
    border Lima on its south and west.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={townships} />
</Article>
